{"title":"Greek letters","markdown":{"headingText":"Greek letters","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-02_greek","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n## Concepts and intutions {.unnumbered}\n\nDo you recognize this?\n\n(zeta  + xi)^2 = zeta^2 + xi^2 + 2 zeta  xi\n\nAfter staring for a while, one might realize that this is identité remarquable.\nIn fact it exactly the same as (a + b) ^ 2 = a^2 + b^2 + 2ab.\n\nThis is the same as 13 + 9 = 22\nBut this is the same as XIII + IX = XXII.\n\nThe romans were not great with maths.\n\nOne challenge is we don't know how to read.\n\nIn short, these are just symbols,  they function as any other notation\ny = a x + b is y =  alpha x + beta.\n\nFor some reasons, they might look more complicated, and to a certain extant more relevant. I am alpha and omega. Is less than I am a and z.\nBut that is not the best way to use them.\n\n## Formal {.unnumbered}\nThe only thing is just to learn the alphabet so here a quick summary\nThere are upper and lower case.\n\nThe other extension is that they are used\nIn physics, constants\nIn math, pi is a recognizable\nIn statistics, they represent something different.\nAs a true parameter.\nThis could facilitate\n\nNeed more symbols\n\n## Examples {.unnumbered}\nFamous letters\nZeta function\nOmega for a set, small for element\nOhm in physics\nrho is for correlation\n\n\n## Tips and warning {.unnumbered}\nepsilon and omicron iota etc are something small\nOmega is used as a set in maths and ohm in physics\n\nNotation for birth is beta and death is delta, or mortality is mu, tau is time.\nOf course, you can but using something intuitive helps.\n\n## if you want to know more {.unnumbered}\nSee @sec-01_operation","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo"